// BY THE WORLD'S CLOCK (where each lies is typed or drawn as said; its moving is a function of the clock alone, and
//   the same world stands at any clock):
//   THE FLIGHT OF FIVE EGRETS round the shoal (SHOAL_FLIGHT, shoalFlightAt). Its round is typed: down the shoal's
//     stream side and back up its lee side, a half round about each end of the shoal. WHERE ON THE ROUND THE FLIGHT
//     STANDS AT THE CLOCK'S NOUGHT THE VISIT'S SEED DRAWS (the stream 'shoal-flight'), as for every other moving
//     body of this world. The whole round keeps 14.6 m and more from the eye's way in space with a wing counted, at
//     every clock; each bird has a body, a neck drawn in, trailing legs and two wings that beat.
//   THE DRIP of each landing's spout into its basin and the rings it makes there; the run and the glint of the
//     water in the channels; the flecks of sun under the cedars, which move as the boughs sway (the shade's law
//     reads the crowns' own places and the wind's clock); the leaves' sway; the boats, the people, the birds, the
//     smokes and the clouds, each on its lane, round, loop or drift.
// BY THE VISIT'S SEED (each kind from a stream of its own name; a garden's planting and a river's traffic are never
//   twice the same, and that is the place's law):
//   THE STAIR'S PLANTING AND THE RIVER WALL'S (SHRUB, TROUGH_SHRUB, POT_TREE; streams 'band-shrubs',
//     'strip-shrubs', 'trough-shrubs', 'pot-trees'): which planter carries one shrub or two, where in its bed each
//     stands, its size, its colour of flower and its turn; the small tree of each river-wall planter; the shrub in
//     each trough; the form, size and turn of each potted tree. The beds, the troughs and the pots are typed. EVERY
//     PLANT IS ROOTED IN ITS BED BEHIND ITS WALL, and what hangs over a coping is the plant's own spread, by up to
//     0.8 m over the stair's parapet and 0.9 m over the river wall's: a ragged line that differs from plant to
//     plant and from terrace to terrace, brick and coping seen between; no curtain of vines, no fall of water,
//     nothing green on a wall's face below that.
//   THE DRIFTS OF FLOWERS on the wood's floor (DRIFTS; streams 'drifts' and one for each drift's clumps): in which
//     place of its bay a drift lies, its breadth and which of the four colours it is (white, crimson, yellow, blue;
//     no kind named). A drift lies only where the wood's own plants, the basins and the paths leave it room.
//   PETALS LYING (PETAL_LIE; stream 'petals-lie') on a west bed's earth: only under a band tree of the last planter
//     that the visit's planting has in flower, since petals lie where a tree flowers.
//   THE CEDARS: their places are the plan's (the pier lines); the five forms (CEDAR_FORMS) are given out in turn
//     along the rows, so that no two neighbours are one form; each form's shape is grown from the visit's stream,
//     and each tree's size and turn drawn from its row's.
//   THE HOUR (the visit's deal: the sun 18, 12 or 6 degrees high) and everything of the plain, the city, the banks,
//     the traffic and the people, as before.
// WHAT IS HANDED THE FLOWN LINE, OR MEASURED FROM IT (said so that nothing of it is hidden):
//   THE FLIGHT'S MARKS (makeFlight: the metre where the lens comes abreast of a standing thing, each by that
//     thing's own constant) give the pace's stretches, the look's turns and the door's revealDistance.
//   SIX OF THE KIT'S FLOCKS (FLOCKS[].keep, KEEP_STRETCH) are handed the way's own points along the stretch their
//     air comes near, as keepClear. IN THIS TREE THAT IS HANDED TO NOTHING: this tree's kit (the older engine)
//     reads no keepClear, so here the six fly their loops as the other flocks do and nothing is steered clear of
//     the way; the owner's engine reads it (the designer's word; we have not run it). What keeps the birds from the
//     way here is where their loops lie: the count takes every flock's whole loop against the whole line by place.
//   THE PEOPLE AND THE BOATS ARE LAID BY PLACES THAT KEEP 20 M AND MORE FROM THE WAY IN SPACE (the owner's rule):
//     nobody on the stair, the quay, the fourth terrace or the top; THE NEAR LANE of the hide boats is r5's in its
//     slots, shifts, thinning and number, and ITS LINE is r6's thread of current a paddle's reach (1.5 m) further
//     out (r7), so that every boatman with his paddle's tip keeps 20 m and more (20.55 m at the least on seeds 1 to
//     400).
//   THREE LAWS OF THE PLACE ARE TYPED FOR THE WAY, EACH THE SAME ON EVERY SEED. (1) THE FOURTH TERRACE'S BAND
//     JUNIPERS ARE AT MOST 0.47 OF THEIR KIND'S FULL SIZE (BAND_JUNIPER_S: 0.42 to 0.47, where it was 0.42 to 0.5
//     before round 8), because the way leaves the stair's middle for the court beside them: so the widest of them
//     keeps the rule of leaves at the eye's height, 4 m, from the lens (4.05 m at the least, by the count). (2) THE
//     PALM STRIP'S FIRST TWO PLANTERS CARRY NO SHRUB (SHRUB.STRIP.FROM): the first planter's length is the low bed
//     with no plant at all, the second carries its palm and its small tree only, and the strip's shrubs stand from
//     the third planter on, because the way comes from the landing onto the stair's foot over the strip's north
//     end: a shrub of the second planter could stand with its crown 1.9 m from the eye's way, under the clear air
//     law's 2.0 m; the third planter's keeps 3.7 m (the law's widest case, the crown taken as a ball of its full
//     reach). (3) THE STORKS' LOOP (FLOCKS, storks) STANDS 24 M UP OVER THE RIVER AND 18 M UP OVER THE MOAT'S NORTH
